{
  "_note": "The class SL-109 closes, as a table (review m1 of the design: the rule over src that keeps a test of the store's not-listed mark out of every other file caught one spelling, a cast of 0x80 or 128 to EntryFlags, and let through the other ways the same always-false test is written). A row is one line of C# as a person writes it into a later view: the cast of the bit to EntryFlags in every literal spelling, Enum.ToObject of it, and a mask of a numeric cast of Flags() with it. expect is fire (at least one pattern of the rule matches), quiet (none does) or residual (a known false negative named in bounded_by: it must stay unmatched, and a row that starts to match is reclassified to fire and struck from bounded_by). before is the number of hits under before_patterns, the one pattern of the first draft, so that a row of expect fire with before 0 is a member the first draft let through. tests/bugs/SL-109-NotListedBitRule.Tests.ps1 reads this file and the patterns from .taad/fitness.yaml, never a copy of them, checks each row, checks before against before_patterns, and checks that no file of src outside the store matches. Rows are spellings a person can write; they were not each put through the compiler, and a spelling that does not compile is still caught.",
  "bounded_by": "Who produces the members: the author of a later view or reader that wants to know, outside the store, whether a folder was listed, and reaches for Flags() because it is the read that is there. Closed grammar for the bit as a number: a literal 128 in decimal, hex (0x, any case, leading zeros, a suffix, digit separators) or binary, or a shift of 1 by 7, that is the operand of a cast to EntryFlags (a qualified or global:: type name, parentheses or an inner numeric cast between), of Enum.ToObject for EntryFlags, or of a bitwise & or &= on either side. Not bounded in, and kept as residual rows: a magnitude or sign test of the flags byte, a 128 that is computed (0x40 << 1, 64 * 2, 0x7F + 1), a name that holds the value, Enum.Parse of the text 128, and a mask written across two statements through a local. The first draft named exactly two spellings. The rule reads src outside ScanStore.cs; a test may name the bit (the store tests do), and only src is fenced.",
  "rule": "no code outside the store spells the not-listed bit as an entry flag",
  "before_patterns": ["\\(EntryFlags\\)\\s*\\(?\\s*(0x80|128)\\b"],
